\f1\b\fs48 \cf2 Resolving the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text V2 Location Paradox for Chirp Models\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
This report provides a comprehensive analysis of the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text (STT) V2 API location requirements, specifically addressing the contradiction encountered when using high-performance models like Chirp in conjunction with regional resource paths and the \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 batch_recognize\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  method. The analysis clarifies the strict coupling between client configuration and resource naming required for V2, details programmatic model discovery, and offers prescriptive Python implementations for regional operations in 2025.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 I. Executive Summary: Resolving the V2 Location Paradox\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 1.1 The Inherent Regionalization of Speech-to-Text V2 and Chirp\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
The Google Cloud Speech-to-Text V2 API was fundamentally designed to meet heightened enterprise security and regulatory needs, focusing specifically on data residency and compliance.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 1
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  This architectural evolution moved away from the V1 API\'92s largely global abstraction toward a strictly regionalized service structure.\
The core promise of V2 is data residency, which ensures that resource generation, logging, and data processing remain entirely within designated geographical boundaries (e.g., Singapore, Belgium, or specific US regions).
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 1
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  This capability is intrinsically tied to the V2 infrastructure.\
The Chirp family of models (\cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 chirp\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 chirp_2\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 chirp_3\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) are Google\'92s advanced, multilingual, LLM-powered Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) systems, available exclusively within V2.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 3
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  Their General Availability (GA) is strictly limited to specific, announced regions, such as \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 us-central1\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 europe-west4\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , and \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 asia-southeast1\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 .
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 3
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  This limitation confirms that Chirp models are inherently regional offerings. Consequently, any deployment requiring Chirp must utilize the regionalized V2 architecture to access the underlying specialized infrastructure.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 1.2 Causal Analysis of the $400$ InvalidArgument Contradiction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
The error message, $400$ Expected resource location to be global, but found us-west1 in resource name, signals a validation failure at the network ingress layer rather than an intrinsic requirement for Chirp to be global.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 7
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  The system's behavior reflects a configuration mismatch between how the client connects to the service and how the resource path is constructed.\
The standard initialization of the Python \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 SpeechClient()\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  often defaults to the global API endpoint (\cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 speech.googleapis.com\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) unless explicitly overridden.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 8
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  This global service ingress layer is configured to route traffic destined for general V2 features and standard models (like \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 long\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) which are allowed to use the global resource path, such as \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 locations/global/recognizers/_\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 .
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 5
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 \
When the global ingress service receives a request that attempts to define a regional resource path (e.g., \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 projects/\{P\}/locations/us-west1/...\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ), it cannot successfully validate or route that request to the corresponding regional control plane. The global endpoint operates outside of that siloed regional boundary and lacks the necessary internal context to guarantee regional execution or data residency commitments. Therefore, the service rejects the request and defaults to demanding conformance with the path it serves, which is \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 global\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 .
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 7
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  The failure occurs because the client attempted to use a regional resource name without first establishing a regional network connection. The resolution is the explicit alignment of the client's network endpoint with the resource\'92s specified region.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 1.3 Immediate Architectural Resolution for Regional Operations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
The solution to the $400$ error mandates establishing the client connection via the corresponding regional service boundary that matches the desired Recognizer resource location.\
To operate successfully with Chirp or any regional V2 resource, the \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 SpeechClient\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  must be initialized using a regional endpoint, formatted as \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 \{REGION\}-speech.googleapis.com\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (e.g., \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 us-central1-speech.googleapis.com\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ).
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 2
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  Subsequently, the Recognizer resource path used in the transcription request must specify the exact matching region (e.g., \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 projects/\{P\}/locations/us-central1/recognizers/_\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ).\
The following table summarizes the mandatory dependency between client configuration and resource path for V2 operations:\
