\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 3.2 Differentiation in Recognition Methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
The user's issue arose during \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 batch_recognize\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 . This recognition method handles long-form audio (one minute up to eight hours) and executes asynchronously via Long Running Operations (LROs).
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 4
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 \
There is no structural difference in location requirements between synchronous (standard) and asynchronous (batch) recognition for the same model string; the model identifier (e.g., \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 chirp\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) remains consistent.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 3
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 \
However, the architecture of batch operations reinforces the need for strict regional enforcement. Batch processing involves storing input data (often via GCS URIs) and deferred computation results.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 12
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  To guarantee data residency, the regional boundary must be enforced not just at the request initiation point but throughout the entire operation's lifecycle, including temporary storage and asynchronous execution. The global entry point cannot provide the necessary regional assurance for LRO integrity, which explains why the validation failure is particularly aggressive when a regional batch request is routed through the default global API endpoint.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

