\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 4.3 Deconstructing the Config Response Object\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \
The response body from \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 client.get_config()\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  contains an instance of the \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Config\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  object (\cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 google.cloud.speech_v2.types.Config\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ).
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 17
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2  While the detailed programmatic schema is typically found within the SDK documentation (e.g., Java documentation references a \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Config\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  class 
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 19
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 ), the API\'92s stated purpose mandates that the object structure must contain specific metadata to fulfill its function of detailing regional capabilities.
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 16
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 \
The \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Config\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  object establishes the formal contract of capabilities for that region. Since the \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 RecognitionConfig\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  object relies on fields like \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 model\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 language_codes\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  
\fs32 \cf3 \cb5 \super \strokec3 20
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \nosupersub \strokec2 , the \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Config\cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  response must provide structured data that validates the availability of these specific parameters within the regional scope. It is inferred that the object contains a key complex field that lists supported capabilities.\
